	'apply' => 'Apply for a place',
	'apply_h' => 'Come and terminate a cable on a Saturday.',
	'apply_p' => 'Open board Saturdays run on the first Saturday of each month from 09:00 to 12:00. Attendance is free: you try a termination with an instructor, in the workshop.',
	'apprenticeship_intro' => 'Our structured pathway combines an intensive twelve-week foundation term at the college with a three-year employer-sponsored apprenticeship on real construction and maintenance sites.',
	'apprenticeship_s1_h' => 'The twelve-week foundation term and tool kit',
	'apprenticeship_s1_p' => 'Before any trainee sets foot on a commercial building site, they must successfully complete our twelve-week foundation term at Foundry Row 9. This full-time introductory term runs five days a week from 07:30 to 16:30. In week one, every trainee receives our comprehensive professional tool kit and personal protective equipment, priced at €620, which becomes their permanent property. Over three months, apprentices practice safe isolation procedures, conduit fabrication, cable tray installation, and distribution board termination. Trainees do not leave for employment until they can independently produce safe, square, and code-compliant installations. The foundation term builds the basic physical discipline and safety consciousness required to enter working sites as a productive, reliable junior electrician from day one.',
	'apprenticeship_s2_h' => 'Year one: containment runs and initial distribution',
	'apprenticeship_s2_p' => 'Once signed with an employer following a successful trial week, Year 1 apprentices establish a routine of four days on site and one day at the college each week. In the first year, training focuses heavily on mechanical containment and fundamental terminations. Trainees learn to measure, cut, bend, and secure galvanised cable tray runs and steel conduit across structural walls. At the college boards, lead instructor Rhea Lindqvist inspects containment alignment, bracket spacing, and the clean dressing of single-core cables into secondary consumer units. To complete Year 1, apprentices must submit their first two signed practical pieces—the distribution board and the cable gland—alongside documented monthly logbooks countersigned by their site supervisor.',
	'apprenticeship_s3_h' => 'Year two: complex distribution boards and sub-mains',
	'apprenticeship_s3_p' => 'Year 2 advances the apprentice into multi-phase distribution and complex commercial installations. On site, trainees assist with larger sub-main cable pulls, busbar chambers, and commercial refits. During their weekly college day, Year 2 students construct complete 14-way distribution boards, ensuring every single circuit is neatly terminated, routed, and clearly labelled. Rhea Lindqvist checks that neutral and earth conductors correspond strictly to protective device numbers without cross-connections. Apprentices also advance their containment techniques, building multi-tier tray systems and precision offset sets in heavy steel conduit. Site assessor Paloma Iturbe conducts regular visits to active job sites, observing the apprentice handling live site coordination, material preparation, and team communication.',
	'apprenticeship_s4_h' => 'Year three: inspection, testing, and fault-finding',
	'apprenticeship_s4_p' => 'Year 3 prepares apprentices for full site autonomy and professional sign-off. Under instructor Kofi Mensah, trainees undertake comprehensive inspection and testing procedures. Working on dedicated three-phase test rigs and motor isolators, students learn to measure earth fault loop impedance, insulation resistance, and prospective short-circuit currents. Every test sheet must be completed by hand with verified mathematical calculations before results are confirmed against calibrated digital meters. Year 3 apprentices master methodical fault-finding techniques, diagnosing open neutrals, high-resistance joints, and earth faults safely. Paloma Iturbe conducts thorough full-day site evaluations, watching the apprentice execute complex tasks on site before their final logbook and six signed pieces are submitted for formal graduation.',
