	'contact_intro' => 'Our admissions office and workshops are located on Foundry Row in Calder Quay, open Monday through Friday for student enquiries and employer partnerships.',
	'contact_s1_h' => 'Foundry Row location and operating hours',
	'contact_s1_p' => 'Akiha College is situated at Foundry Row 9, Calder Quay. The workshop bays operate Monday through Friday from 07:30 to 16:30, while our admissions and administrative office is open Monday through Friday from 09:00 to 17:00. The college is closed on Sundays and public holidays. Visitors arriving by public transit or bicycle can access our yard directly from the quayside lane, where secure bike racks are located near the main entrance. You can contact our team by calling +00 20 7410 0388 or by emailing hello@akiha-college.example. All admissions enquiries receive a direct written response within two business days.',
	'contact_s2_h' => 'How do I apply for an upcoming intake?',
	'contact_s2_p' => 'Applications begin by attending a free Open board Saturday or submitting an enquiry form directly to our admissions office. After an initial interview and verification of basic mechanical aptitude, candidates apply for either the January intake (starts 11 January 2027, applications close 27 November 2026) or September intake (starts 6 September 2027, applications close 4 June 2027). Each cohort is strictly capped at twenty-four places. Successful applicants enroll in the twelve-week foundation term before undergoing their employer trial week and formal three-year apprenticeship contract.',
	'contact_s3_h' => 'Who pays for the three-year apprenticeship tuition?',
	'contact_s3_p' => 'Tuition for the three-year apprenticeship is €4,800 per year, paid directly by the partner employer once an apprentice passes their foundation term, completes the trial week, and signs an employment contract. The initial twelve-week foundation term tuition of €2,400 and the personal tool kit and safety gear cost of €620 are paid by the student or their self-funding sponsor prior to commencement. Trainees do not incur tuition costs for the subsequent three years of site and college training while employed.',
	'contact_s4_h' => 'What happens if a practical piece is not signed off?',
	'contact_s4_p' => 'If a practical assignment fails to meet required standards—such as an improperly stripped cable, incorrect terminal torque, or misaligned conduit—the instructor will not sign the assessment sheet. The apprentice must disassemble the work, correct the error, and re-terminate the piece until it complies fully with safety regulations. A practical re-assessment fee of €180 applies to cover additional bench supervision and materials. No apprentice advances to the next stage of training until all mandatory signed pieces are completed satisfactorily.',
	'contact_s5_h' => 'How are trainees matched with partner employers?',
	'contact_s5_p' => 'Akiha College maintains partnerships with forty-one electrical contractors, commercial facilities maintenance teams, and engineering firms across Calder Quay. Once a trainee completes the twelve-week foundation term and secures their first two signed pieces—the distribution board and the armoured gland—site liaison Paloma Iturbe coordinates placement for a five-day trial week. On day 3, Paloma visits the active site to assess compatibility. If both employer and trainee agree, a three-year contract is executed with four days on site and one day at college.',
	'contact_s6_h' => 'What tools and equipment are provided?',
	'contact_s6_p' => 'In week one of the foundation term, every enrolled student receives our comprehensive professional tool kit and personal protective equipment for a fee of €620. This kit includes insulated screwdrivers, pliers, side cutters, cable strippers, measuring tools, a spirit level, and safety eyewear. These tools are issued for permanent personal ownership and will be used throughout the foundation term and subsequent years on site. The college workshop provides heavy tooling, pipe benders, conduit stocks, power tools, and calibrated test instruments.',
	'contact_s7_h' => 'Can I inspect the workshop before submitting an application?',
	'contact_s7_p' => 'Yes. We encourage all prospective students, parents, and employers to visit our converted foundry shed before applying. Our Open board Saturdays take place on the first Saturday of each month from 09:00 to 12:00 and are completely free to attend. You can tour our four workshop bays, observe current training boards, speak directly with founder Ines Varga and instructors Rhea Lindqvist and Kofi Mensah, and try your hand at terminating an armoured cable under professional supervision.',
