	'how_we_judge_intro' => 'We assess electrical competence through tangible physical workmanship, meticulous hand-written test documentation, and unannounced full-day inspections on active job sites.',
	'how_we_judge_s1_h' => 'The six signed pieces of evidence',
	'how_we_judge_s1_p' => 'An academic grade cannot tell an employer whether an electrician can safely terminate a three-phase board or gland an armoured cable in wet conditions. Therefore, Akiha College evaluates every year of training through six mandatory signed pieces. Piece 01 is the distribution board, built, terminated, and neatly labelled. Piece 02 is the tray run, requiring containment to be measured, fixed, and neatly dressed. Piece 03 is the gland, where steel-wire armoured cable is stripped, glanded, and torque-checked with a calibrated spanner. Piece 04 is the test sheet, written legibly by hand and verified against calibrated meter readings. Piece 05 is the site visit, an in-person assessment of an active work day. Piece 06 is the employer logbook, countersigned monthly by site supervisors. Only when all six pieces bear valid instructor signatures is a year certified.',
	'how_we_judge_s2_h' => 'Three full-day site visits every year',
	'how_we_judge_s2_p' => 'Site assessor Paloma Iturbe visits every apprentice on their employer’s active job sites three times each training year. These are not superficial twenty-minute check-ins. Paloma spends a full working day shadowing the apprentice, observing everything from morning site briefings and safe isolation routines to tool handling, material staging, and housekeeping. She verifies that the apprentice is given appropriate electrical installation responsibilities rather than being sidelined with menial labor. At the end of the shift, Paloma reviews the day’s work alongside the employer’s site supervisor, discussing progress, correcting minor technical deficiencies, and signing off the site assessment piece only when standards are fully met.',
	'how_we_judge_s3_h' => 'Re-assessment and substandard workmanship',
	'how_we_judge_s3_p' => 'We never grant passing marks for substandard electrical work. If a terminal screw is loose, an armoured cable gland is improperly torqued, or a containment bracket is misaligned, the piece is rejected without compromise. The instructor marks the defect and explains the mechanical or safety failure. The apprentice must strip the assembly down, salvage or replace materials, and rebuild the piece from scratch. A formal practical re-assessment costs €180 to cover instructor bench time and materials. This uncompromising policy teaches trainees that on live commercial and domestic sites, loose connections and poor isolations cause fires and equipment damage. Standards must be met on the board before work can be trusted in the field.',
