	'tagline' => 'Electrical apprenticeship college · Since 1998 · Calder Quay',
	'trial_h' => 'A week on your site, with a visit on day three.',
	'trial_p' => 'Before a contract is signed the trainee spends five working days with your team. The assessor visits on day three, watches the work and talks with you both.',
	'watch1_body' => 'Every turn of the torque spanner matters when securing armoured cable into an isolator. The instructor watches how the brass threads catch, whether the armour wires are trimmed clean without scoring the inner sheath, and how firmly the earth tag is anchored. A gland either seals true or fails under pressure.',
	'watch1_lead' => 'The gland.',
	'watch2_body' => 'Neat circuit labels reveal how an electrician thinks about whoever opens the distribution board next. The instructor checks that each neutral and live conductor corresponds exactly to its breaker marking, cut square and dressed straight down the trunking without crossing. If a board cannot be understood at a glance, it is not finished.',
	'watch2_lead' => 'The labels.',
	'watch3_body' => 'Good installation habits show in the way tools are handled long before meters are switched on. The instructor watches for deliberate isolation checks, steady wire stripping that leaves no copper nicked, and continuous awareness of posture and ladder safety. Methodical discipline cannot be faked when someone is standing beside your board.',
	'watch3_lead' => 'The hands.',
	'watch_h' => 'What the instructor watches',
	'week_c1_d' => 'On the employer’s site, beside a supervisor.',
	'week_c1_t' => 'Four days',
	'week_c2_d' => 'Back at the college, at the training boards.',
	'week_c2_t' => 'One day',
	'week_c3_d' => 'Six signed pieces in each year.',
	'week_c3_t' => 'Three years',
	'week_h' => 'Four days on site, one day here.',
	'week_p' => 'Every week a trainee works four days with the employer and returns on the fifth to the college workshop, where an instructor signs what was built.',
	'work_intro' => 'We evaluate our training by the durability, precision, and neatness of the installations our apprentices leave behind on active customer sites.',
	'work_s1_h' => 'Bakery refit, Calder Quay — Joel Haddad',
	'work_s1_p' => 'Working on a commercial bakery refit in Calder Quay, Year 2 apprentice Joel Haddad undertook the complete assembly and termination of a new 14-way distribution board. In a demanding catering environment where moisture, flour dust, and continuous machinery loads are present, neat cable routing and unambiguous circuit identification are essential. Joel dressed each circuit cleanly into the enclosure, securing conductors with uniform spacing and labelling every outgoing breaker by hand and terminal tag. Lead instructor Rhea Lindqvist visited the installation to inspect terminal torque settings, phase identification, and earth continuity before formally signing off the board as completed.',
	'work_s2_h' => 'School sports hall — Amira Sesay',
	'work_s2_p' => 'During a major refurbishment of a school sports hall, Year 1 apprentice Amira Sesay installed a continuous 38-metre run of galvanised cable tray suspended at a height of four metres. Working safely from mobile podium steps, Amira measured, fabricated, and secured multiple bracket supports across the steel structural columns. She formed neat factory-quality factory bends and level joints along the full length of the wall. Cables were dressed with uniform spacing and strapped cleanly to withstand structural vibration. Rhea Lindqvist reviewed the completed containment run on site, inspecting bracket load ratings and tray alignment before signing the assessment sheet.',
	'work_s3_h' => 'Housing co-op car park — Sven Aalto',
	'work_s3_p' => 'At a community housing co-op car park, Year 3 apprentice Sven Aalto was tasked with installing a dedicated vehicle charging circuit. The installation required running heavy armoured cable along external brickwork and terminating it securely into an industrial isolator next to a wall-mounted charger. Sven stripped the outer protective sheath, formed a clean brass gland termination with correct armor clamping, and torqued all mechanical joints to specification. Site assessor Paloma Iturbe conducted a formal site observation during the installation, watching Sven verify the earth loop path and weatherproofing seals before adding her signature to his practical log.',
	'work_s4_h' => 'Lift motor room — Noor Rahimi',
	'work_s4_p' => 'In the motor room of a high-rise lift installation, Year 3 apprentice Noor Rahimi conducted diagnostic fault-finding and complete electrical verification on a commercial motor isolator. Working systematically with calibrated test instruments, Noor traced an intermittent control circuit fault, verifying continuity and insulation integrity across all phases. Following the repair, she compiled an inspection test sheet entirely by hand, recording exact meter readings and resistance calculations. Instructor Kofi Mensah reviewed her diagnostic logic and audited the hand-written test documentation against digital meter logs before signing off the completed piece.',
	'work_s5_h' => 'The six pieces required for annual completion',
	'work_s5_p' => 'Every apprentice\'s progress is measured against six concrete pieces of evidence produced each year. These six deliverables must be submitted in strict sequence: the distribution board, the cable tray containment run, the armoured cable gland, the hand-written test sheet, the assessor\'s site visit evaluation, and the monthly countersigned employer logbook. Instructors inspect each piece in person. There are no automated assessments, multiple-choice shortcuts, or surrogate assignments. A year of apprenticeship is only certified when all six signed pieces have been inspected, approved, and filed in the student’s permanent record.',
	'yrs_h' => 'Three years, three signed sets of pieces',
);
